# Backend Conventions

## sql.js Query Pattern

`sql.js` returns `{columns: string[], values: any[][]}[]`. Always use the `mapRows` helper:

```typescript
function mapRows(results: any[]): Record<string, any>[] {
  if (!results.length || !results[0].values.length) return [];
  const columns = results[0].columns;
  return results[0].values.map((row: any[]) => {
    const obj: Record<string, any> = {};
    columns.forEach((col: string, i: number) => { obj[col] = row[i]; });
    return obj;
  });
}
```

## API Response Shapes (must match frontend expectations)

- `GET /committees` → returns **array** directly (not `{committees:[]}`)
- `GET /committees/:id` → returns **flat object** with `.members[]` and `.officers[]` at top level
- `GET /officers` → returns `{officers:[], total, page, limit}`
- `GET /officers/:id` → returns `{officer: {...}}`
- `GET /evaluations/committee/:id/status` → returns **array** of officer status items
- `GET /reports/committee/:id/decisions-card` → returns `{committee, members[], officers[]}`
- `GET /reports/committee/:id/credentials` → returns **array** directly

## Officer Field Names

Officers table uses `per_name` (not `name`), `rank_code` (not `rank_id`), `unit_code`, `job_code`. Joins add `rank_name`, `unit_name`, `job_name`.

## Auth Middleware

All routes use `authenticate` middleware. Admin-only routes add `requireAdmin`. The user object is on `req.user` with `{id, username, role, display_name}`.

## Always call `saveDB()` after any write operation (INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE).
